Answer: An Armstrong number is equal to the sum of its digits raised to the power of the total number of digits. For example: 153 = 1³ + 5³ + 3³ Print Invalid input when the number is negative. Create two classes named ArmstrongChecker and Main. ArmstrongChecker must contain a method named checkArmstrong() that accepts an integer. Use while loops to count and process the digits. Use a for loop to calculate the power of each digit. Print Armstrong number when the condition is satisfied. Otherwise, print Not an Armstrong number. In main(), create an ArmstrongChecker object and call checkArmstrong(). Input Format A single integer. Output Format Print Armstrong number, Not an Armstrong number, or Invalid input. Sample Input1: 153 Sample Output1: Armstrong number Sample Input2: 123 Sample Output2: Not an Armstrong number

```java
import java.util.Scanner;
class ArmstrongChecker {
int countDigits(int number) {
if (number == 0) {
return 1;
}
int count = 0;
while (number > 0) {
count++;
number = number / 10;
}
return count;
}
int calculatePower(int base, int exponent) {
int result = 1;
for (int count = 1; count <= exponent; count++) {
result = result * base;
}
return result;
}
```

void checkArmstrong(int number) {

```java
if (number < 0) {
System.out.println("Invalid input");
return;
}
int originalNumber = number;
int numberOfDigits = countDigits(number);
int sum = 0;
while (number > 0) {
int digit = number % 10;
sum = sum + calculatePower(digit, numberOfDigits);
number = number / 10;
}
if (sum == originalNumber) {
System.out.println("Armstrong number");
```

} else {

```java
System.out.println("Not an Armstrong number");
}
}
}
public class Main {
public static void main(String[] args) {
Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
int number = scanner.nextInt();
ArmstrongChecker checker = new ArmstrongChecker();
checker.checkArmstrong(number);
scanner.close();
}
}
```
